<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>

<overview>
<p>
The Iron web framework builds a response from a tuple of modifiers, for example
<code>Response::with((status::Ok, body))</code>. A body that is added this way is sent without a
<code>Content-Type</code> header unless one of the modifiers sets it, and browsers then guess the
content type from the body. If the body contains user-controlled data, an attacker can make the
browser render it as HTML and run a script in the context of the application.
</p>
<p>
This query finds user-controlled data that is part of a modifier tuple passed to
<code>Response::with</code>, including the elements of tuples that are nested in it. A response
whose modifiers include a content type parsed from a string literal that does not name HTML or
XML, such as <code>"text/plain".parse::&lt;Mime&gt;().unwrap()</code>, and none that does, is not
reported.
</p>
<p>
A database that is built without compiling the code can leave the calls into Iron without a target,
when the names come from a glob import such as <code>use iron::prelude::*</code>. In a file that
uses <code>iron</code>, the query also recognizes these calls by name. A function or closure with a
parameter of type <code>Request</code>, <code>&amp;Request</code> or <code>&amp;mut Request</code> is
a source, the <code>url</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>body</code> and <code>extensions</code>
fields of the request and the methods that are called on it are followed, and a call of
<code>Response::with</code> that has no target is a sink. For such a call, a body that is passed on its
own, as in <code>Response::with(body)</code>, is a sink as well, because a body that is set on its own
has no content type.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Set an explicit content type that is not HTML when the body is not markup, or encode the
user-controlled data for HTML before it is added to the body.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
In the following example, the path of the request is written into the body of the response without
a content type, so a path that contains markup is rendered as HTML:
</p>
<sample src="IronResponseXssBad.rs" />
<p>
In the following corrected example, the response declares that its body is plain text:
</p>
<sample src="IronResponseXssGood.rs" />
</example>

<section title="Known limitations">
<p>
A content type that is set by another kind of modifier, such as <code>Header(ContentType::json())</code>,
is not recognized, so the body of such a response is still reported. A content type is recognized
only when it is parsed directly from a string literal and flows to the tuple through local
variables, <code>unwrap</code>, <code>expect</code> or <code>?</code>. The type that the literal is
parsed into is not checked. If several parsed literals can reach the content types of a response, for
example one on each branch of an <code>if</code>, or one in each of two modifiers, the response is
recognized only when none of them names HTML or XML. Iron applies the modifiers in order, so a
<code>text/html</code> modifier after a <code>text/plain</code> one replaces it, and the query does
not try to tell which of two content types wins.
</p>
<p>
The sinks are the expressions that a modifier tuple is built from, so a tuple that is changed after it
is built, for example by assigning a body to one of its fields (<code>modifiers.1 = body;</code>), is
not tracked.
</p>
<p>
When the calls have no target, the query goes by names and only looks at files that use
<code>iron</code>. It follows the result of every method that is called on the request and has no
target, except for a few that only tell a property of the value, such as <code>len</code> and
<code>is_empty</code>, and for names that say that they escape or encode for HTML, such as
<code>escape_html</code>, which are taken to do so. A method that encodes the value under another name
(or only partly, as <code>escape_default</code> does) is not recognized as a barrier. A parameter is
recognized by the name of its type, so it can be a type of another crate that has the same name. A
function that takes a request is a source whether or not a call of <code>Iron::new</code> or
<code>Chain::new</code> registers it. A body that is passed on its own to a call of
<code>Response::with</code> that resolves is reported by this query only when the standard query does
not report it: the handler is not registered in the file, or a call that has no target lies between the
request and the body.
</p>
</section>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/xss/">Cross Site Scripting (XSS)</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html">CWE-79</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
